=========================
FRAMES / IFRAMES IN PLAYWRIGHT
=========================

WHAT IS AN IFRAME?
------------------

An iframe (Inline Frame) is a webpage inside another webpage.

Think of it as:

Main Page
    ↓
Contains another mini webpage

That mini webpage is called an iframe.

--------------------------------------------------

REAL LIFE EXAMPLES
------------------

1. Payment Gateway

Website
    ↓
Payment Form (inside iframe)

2. YouTube Video Embed

Website
    ↓
Embedded YouTube Player (iframe)

3. Google Maps Embed

Website
    ↓
Google Map (iframe)

4. Chat Widgets

Website
    ↓
Chat Support Window (iframe)

--------------------------------------------------

WHY DO WE NEED SPECIAL HANDLING?
--------------------------------

Suppose page contains:

Main Page
    ↓
iframe
    ↓
Username Textbox

If we try:

await page.locator('#username').fill('Kirti');

Playwright may fail.

Why?

Because username textbox is NOT present
on the main page.

It is inside iframe.

--------------------------------------------------

IMPORTANT CONCEPT
-----------------

Main Page and Iframe are different DOMs.

Main Page DOM
    ↓
Contains iframe

Iframe DOM
    ↓
Contains actual elements

Therefore:

page.locator()

cannot directly access iframe elements.

--------------------------------------------------

HOW TO HANDLE IFRAMES?
----------------------

Playwright provides:

1. frameLocator()  ⭐ Most Common
2. frame()

--------------------------------------------------

METHOD 1: frameLocator()
------------------------

Recommended Approach

Syntax:

const frame =
page.frameLocator('iframe-selector');

--------------------------------------------------

EXAMPLE
-------

HTML:

<iframe id="login-frame"></iframe>

Inside iframe:

<input id="username">

Playwright:

const frame =
page.frameLocator('#login-frame');

await frame
.locator('#username')
.fill('Kirti');

--------------------------------------------------

UNDERSTANDING THE FLOW
----------------------

Step 1

Locate iframe

const frame =
page.frameLocator('#login-frame');

Step 2

Locate element inside iframe

frame.locator('#username')

Step 3

Perform action

.fill('Kirti')

--------------------------------------------------

COMPLETE EXAMPLE
----------------

const frame =
page.frameLocator('#login-frame');

await frame
.locator('#username')
.fill('standard_user');

await frame
.locator('#password')
.fill('secret_sauce');

await frame
.locator('#login-button')
.click();

--------------------------------------------------

METHOD 2: frame()
-----------------

Alternative Approach

Syntax:

const frame =
page.frame({ name: 'frame-name' });

--------------------------------------------------

EXAMPLE
-------

const frame =
page.frame({ name: 'login-frame' });

await frame?.locator('#username')
.fill('Kirti');

--------------------------------------------------

WHY frameLocator() IS PREFERRED?
--------------------------------

frameLocator()

✔ Cleaner
✔ Auto waits
✔ Easier to read

Therefore in real projects:

Mostly use frameLocator()

--------------------------------------------------

HOW TO IDENTIFY AN IFRAME?
--------------------------

Open Browser DevTools

Look for:

<iframe>

Example:

<iframe
id="payment-frame">
</iframe>

If you see iframe tag

→ Element is inside iframe.

--------------------------------------------------

NESTED IFRAMES
--------------

Sometimes:

Main Page
    ↓
Iframe 1
    ↓
Iframe 2
    ↓
Textbox

This is called Nested Iframe.

--------------------------------------------------

PLAYWRIGHT HANDLING
-------------------

const innerFrame =
page
.frameLocator('#frame1')
.frameLocator('#frame2');

await innerFrame
.locator('#username')
.fill('Kirti');

--------------------------------------------------

VERIFY ELEMENT INSIDE IFRAME
----------------------------

const frame =
page.frameLocator('#login-frame');

await expect(
frame.locator('#username')
).toBeVisible();

--------------------------------------------------

CLICK INSIDE IFRAME
-------------------

const frame =
page.frameLocator('#login-frame');

await frame
.locator('#submit')
.click();

--------------------------------------------------

READ TEXT FROM IFRAME
---------------------

const frame =
page.frameLocator('#login-frame');

const text =
await frame
.locator('.message')
.textContent();

--------------------------------------------------

COMMON MISTAKE
--------------

Wrong:

await page
.locator('#username')
.fill('Kirti');

Reason:

Element is inside iframe.

--------------------------------------------------

Correct:

const frame =
page.frameLocator('#login-frame');

await frame
.locator('#username')
.fill('Kirti');

--------------------------------------------------

INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
-------------------

Q. What is an iframe?

Answer:

An iframe is a webpage embedded inside another webpage.

--------------------------------------------------

Q. Why can't page.locator() access iframe elements?

Answer:

Because iframe has its own DOM which is separate from the main page DOM.

--------------------------------------------------

Q. How do you handle iframes in Playwright?

Answer:

Using:

1. frameLocator()
2. frame()

--------------------------------------------------

Q. Which method is preferred?

Answer:

frameLocator()

because it is cleaner and supports auto waiting.

--------------------------------------------------

Q. How do you identify an iframe?

Answer:

Inspect the page and look for the <iframe> tag.

